// The TLS 1.3 key schedule, RFC 8446 §7.1 — and the first consumer `secret` has ever had.
//
// `vision/packages/crypto/src/secret.wac` proposes a qualifier that propagates like `const`, and its
// two uses are both inside that file. Nothing outside `crypto` has ever received a propagated one,
// so nothing has tested whether it composes. This file is eleven functions that pass a secret to
// each other, which is what the proposal is for, and it does not survive them.
//
// The chain, from the real file's own diagram:
//
//                       0
//     PSK ------> HKDF-Extract  = Early Secret
//                 Derive-Secret("derived", "")
//     (EC)DHE --> HKDF-Extract  = Handshake Secret  --> "c hs traffic", "s hs traffic"
//                 Derive-Secret("derived", "")
//     0 --------> HKDF-Extract  = Master Secret     --> "c ap traffic", "s ap traffic", "res master"
//
// Every arrow is *secret in, secret out*. That is the sentence the proposal cannot write.

import { Bytes } from "core";
import { sha256 } from "@/packages/crypto/src/sha256.wac";
import { hkdfExtract, hkdfExpand } from "@/packages/crypto/src/hkdf.wac";
import { hmacSha256 } from "@/packages/crypto/src/hmac.wac";

export i32 hashLen() { return 32; }

/**
 * ## 1. A qualifier on a parameter cannot say what a function returns
 *
 * **Written the other way first, and the parser refused it.** Every signature below was
 * `export secret u8[] deriveSecret(…)` until `tools/specparse.ts` answered
 * `keyschedule.wac:49: no rule reaches '['` — because `GRAMMAR.ebnf` says
 * `param = [ "const" ] , [ "secret" ] , type , IDENT` and a return type is not a parameter. The
 * grammar was written from the proposal and the proposal has no return position, so the refusal is
 * the proposal's own answer rather than an oversight in the notation. It is also the first time the
 * parser has settled a *design* question instead of a spelling.
 *
 * `secret` is modelled on `const`, and `const` is a **parameter and receiver** qualifier: a flag on
 * a name in the checker's scope table, which is why `vision/QUESTIONS.md` records that constness is
 * not part of the type and why `issues/lang/open/0315a`'s five leaks are one fact. Inheriting that
 * machinery inherits its shape, and the shape has no return position.
 *
 * So this is writable:
 *
 *     u8[] deriveSecret(secret u8[] from, Bytes label, Bytes transcript)
 *
 * and it is wrong. The value coming out is the next secret in the chain, and it is untainted the
 * moment it is returned — `handshakeSecret(earlySecret(psk), dhe)` launders at the first arrow. The
 * qualifier survives exactly one call and the chain is ten.
 *
 * **This is not a gap that a return-position annotation obviously fixes**, which is why it is the
 * first thing here rather than a footnote. `secret u8[] deriveSecret(...)` reads fine and means
 * something the checker cannot represent: `const` is a fact about *what this function may do to a
 * name it was lent*, and a return value is not lent to anyone. Making `secret` work in return
 * position makes it a property of the value, which is a property of the *type* — and the whole
 * argument for `secret` was that it costs nothing because the machinery is already there.
 */
export u8[] deriveSecret(secret u8[] from, Bytes label, Bytes transcript) { … }

export u8[] earlySecret(secret u8[] psk) { … }
export u8[] handshakeSecret(secret u8[] early, secret u8[] dhe) { … }
export u8[] masterSecret(secret u8[] handshake) { … }
export u8[] nextTrafficSecret(secret u8[] from) { … }

/**
 * ## 2. Half an array is secret and the other half is public
 *
 * The shipped version returns key ++ iv in one `u8[]`, and says why: *"wac has no tuples and a
 * struct here would be a type crossing the bindgen boundary for no benefit."*
 *
 * A key is secret. **An IV is not** — it is derived, it is not sensitive, and in TLS it is XORed
 * with a visible sequence number. One array cannot be half-tainted, so the concatenation forces a
 * choice: mark the whole thing secret and the IV can never reach a record header, or mark none of it
 * and the key is loose.
 *
 * Returning them separately is the answer here and it is worth noticing *why the shipped code did
 * not*: the reason it gives is tuples, and the reason it would need now is taint. A qualifier that
 * cannot describe part of a value pushes back on the data layout, which is a cost the proposal never
 * mentions because it never had a consumer whose values were mixed.
 */
export struct TrafficKeys { u8[] key; u8[] iv; }   // `secret` is not a field qualifier either
export TrafficKeys trafficKeyIv(secret u8[] from, i32 keyLen) { … }

/**
 * ## 3. The Finished message is a declassification, and it is correct
 *
 * `finishedVerify` takes a secret and produces sixteen bytes that go **on the wire**. That is the
 * point of it — it proves the sender holds the handshake secret and saw the same transcript.
 *
 * `crypto/src/secret.wac` says *"a laundered `secret` is a key in a log line"*, and treats laundering
 * as the failure mode. Here laundering is the feature. So the proposal needs a way to say *this
 * derivation is a deliberate declassification*, and there is no such thing in it — which leaves
 * exactly two outcomes, both bad: `finishedVerify` is written without the qualifier and the taint
 * never enters the function, or it is written with one and every TLS handshake needs an escape
 * hatch on its most security-critical line.
 *
 * **A taint system without a declassification form is a taint system nobody can finish using.** That
 * is not a subtle point and it is not in the proposal, and the reason is that its two examples —
 * `chachaBlock` and `aesEncrypt` — are the two functions in cryptography that genuinely never
 * declassify.
 */
export u8[] finishedVerify(secret u8[] baseKey, Bytes transcriptHash) { … }

/**
 * The label expansion everything above goes through, and the one function whose signature the
 * proposal gets exactly right: a secret in, a length, a label that is public, a secret out.
 *
 * Which is the honest note to end on. `secret` is right about `expandLabel`, and `expandLabel` is
 * the shape its two crypto examples have. Eleven functions later it is right about one of them.
 */
export u8[] expandLabel(secret u8[] from, Bytes label, Bytes context, i32 length) { … }
